00:28:13,435 --> 00:28:14,560
Tomatoes. Same.

474
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:17,345
Carbonated drinks. Okay?

475
00:28:17,345 --> 00:28:24,160
They will pick a carbonated drink by going through this process.

476
00:28:26,780 --> 00:28:29,190
1, 2, 3, 4. Okay?

477
00:28:29,190 --> 00:28:33,535
1, 2, 3, 4. That is my best understanding of it on how they pick this stuff.

478
00:28:33,535 --> 00:28:40,585
And then every, I think it's every 4 years, they switch the item.

479
00:28:40,585 --> 00:28:44,480
So it won't be a red apple. Next time it might be a different type of apple. Okay?

480
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:49,520
Because obviously they wanna... Because what happens is, consumers do change their behaviour.

481
00:28:49,520 --> 00:29:01,760
And this is one reason that the Fed prefers to use the... I think it's the PCE price index. Okay?

482
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:03,395
We can have a brief chat about that.

483
00:29:03,395 --> 00:29:05,585
Versus the CPI. I'm not that bothered.

484
00:29:05,585 --> 00:29:07,320
I like to look at the CPI.

485
00:29:07,320 --> 00:29:07,980
It really doesn't matter.

486
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:09,505
They move very closely together.

487
00:29:09,505 --> 00:29:12,835
But the Fed... Do you remember I said in the last section?

488
00:29:12,835 --> 00:29:18,200
That the Fed has other preferred, slightly different to the CPI, that they like to look at.

489
00:29:18,200 --> 00:29:22,870
So there... I think the 2% target is actually based on the PCE bar.

490
00:29:22,870 --> 00:29:26,095
To be honest, most traders I know prefer the CPI.

491
00:29:26,095 --> 00:29:27,920
It's released early in the month.

492
00:29:29,420 --> 00:29:32,140
And actually the CPI is very important as well.

493
00:29:32,140 --> 00:29:38,970
It's very reflective because the CPI will be used to adjust social security payments, pension payments.

494
00:29:38,970 --> 00:29:42,980
And you know, it's used as an index to make certain payments to the population.

495
00:29:42,980 --> 00:29:46,530
So it's a very good representation.

496
00:29:46,530 --> 00:29:48,600
But there is a slight difference.

497
00:29:48,600 --> 00:29:53,715
So with the CPI, these baskets are not updated as often with the PCE.

498
00:29:53,715 --> 00:29:57,185
The PCE is done by the BEA, I believe.

499
00:29:57,185 --> 00:30:01,090
And they actually use these same prices from the BLS.

500
00:30:01,090 --> 00:30:05,275
So they take the price in the BLS, but they update the basket a little bit more.

501
00:30:05,275 --> 00:30:07,120
And the reason for that is substitution.

502
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:09,420
So if you think about it, if the price of...

503
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:12,930
Let's say they're using red apples. Here. Okay.

504
00:30:12,930 --> 00:30:16,855
And I think the example that I read on the website...

505
00:30:16,855 --> 00:30:18,765
So for example, for cheese.

506
00:30:18,765 --> 00:30:24,610
I think the example that the BLS give in the Q&A that we're going to have a look at.

507
00:30:24,610 --> 00:30:27,060
I think it's cheddar cheese or something.

508
00:30:28,780 --> 00:30:30,110
So it could be cheddar cheese.

509
00:30:30,110 --> 00:30:33,880
It could be red apples. Whatever it is. Okay.

510
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:37,415
Even if it's milk. In fact, they use whole milk there.

511
00:30:37,415 --> 00:30:39,340
I was going to say semi-skimmed or whatever, but...

512
00:30:39,340 --> 00:30:40,230
Do you see my point?

513
00:30:40,230 --> 00:30:45,425
They may have a different item representing one of these categories. Okay.

514
00:30:45,425 --> 00:30:49,905
Let's say the price of red apples... So let's say it jumped 10% in a month.

515
00:30:49,905 --> 00:30:52,190
Are people going to continue buying red apples?

516
00:30:52,190 --> 00:30:52,350
No.

517
00:30:52,350 --> 00:30:57,015
They might switch to green apples. They might switch to a different type of apple. Do you see?

518
00:30:57,015 --> 00:31:06,300
And this won't get updated for quite some time. Whereas the PCE have some methods to reflect that. Okay.

519
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:07,820
It's not... I mean, it's not greatly...

520
00:31:07,820 --> 00:31:12,060
Obviously, it's difficult to change as often as they would like.

521
00:31:12,060 --> 00:31:19,965
But they just update these categories weights more often to reflect changes in substitution. You know?

522
00:31:19,965 --> 00:31:23,160
Or people might not even... They may just stop buying apples altogether.

523
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:26,874
If apples go up and some other substitute fruit like

524
00:31:26,874 --> 00:31:29,474
bananas becomes relatively cheaper, they might start

525
00:31:29,474 --> 00:31:33,188
buying more bananas. And do you see what I mean?

526
00:31:33,190 --> 00:31:34,360
Behaviors change.

527
00:31:34,360 --> 00:31:38,240
So these weightings need to be updated ideally every month.

528
00:31:38,240 --> 00:31:45,385
But obviously, that survey that you saw, the consumer... Is it the expenditures survey?

529
00:31:45,385 --> 00:31:49,040
Annual expenditures... This one, wasn't it?

530
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:52,400
I can't... I keep forgetting what

531
00:31:52,400 --> 00:31:53,493
it's called. Yeah, the

532
00:31:53,493 --> 00:31:54,859
consumer... I had it right

533
00:31:54,859 --> 00:31:56,499
the first time. Consumer Expenditure Survey.

534
00:31:56,500 --> 00:31:58,810
Don't carry that out every month, right?

535
00:31:58,810 --> 00:32:00,025
Talking thousands of people.

536
00:32:00,025 --> 00:32:01,670
Obviously, it's not realistic.

537
00:32:01,670 --> 00:32:04,889
Whereas the VA do have a way of

538
00:32:04,889 --> 00:32:07,303
updating the weightings much more often.

539
00:32:07,303 --> 00:32:10,119
That's why the Fed slightly prefer it.

540
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:10,975
There is another reason.

541
00:32:10,975 --> 00:32:13,930
They have a broader basket. But let's not get into that now.

542
00:32:13,930 --> 00:32:16,415
Because as a trader, I really don't care.

543
00:32:16,415 --> 00:32:20,115
I'm not here, like I said, to argue about which one is better.

544
00:32:20,115 --> 00:32:21,805
They're both roughly the same.

545
00:32:21,805 --> 00:32:24,185
And I'm going to show you they give very similar results.

546
00:32:24,185 --> 00:32:25,240
That's all I care about.

547
00:32:27,220 --> 00:32:33,915
Whether one has potatoes and one should include cucumbers. You see, it's starting to get silly.

548
00:32:33,915 --> 00:32:36,300
You have to... This is why I say, don't make that fatal error.

549
00:32:36,300 --> 00:32:38,560
I've seen people make that fatal error before.

550
00:32:38,560 --> 00:32:43,115
But I've also seen people... I've actually seen the majority of people make the other fatal error.

551
00:32:43,115 --> 00:32:45,145
And I hate to say it. And I am going to say it.

552
00:32:45,145 --> 00:32:50,770
When you watch these videos on YouTube, social media. The foggiest what inflation really is.

553
00:32:50,770 --> 00:32:53,865
So it's better to know too much than too little.

554
00:32:53,865 --> 00:32:56,880
Obviously, don't make the fatal error of knowing nothing.

555
00:32:56,880 --> 00:32:59,690
That's why I am... You need to know this stuff, at least.

556
00:32:59,690 --> 00:33:02,040
But then don't go too far. Have the balance.

557
00:33:02,040 --> 00:33:05,285
Don't know too little, which is what most people know.

558
00:33:05,285 --> 00:33:08,175
They don't even have a clue that there's these categories.

559
00:33:08,175 --> 00:33:11,780
But now that you know that, you don't need to...

560
00:33:11,780 --> 00:33:14,560
It's not like with the FOMC, with the FAIRD FOMC.

561
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:17,970
I want you to watch that section five times, ten times, a hundred times.

562
00:33:17,970 --> 00:33:23,380
I want you to go to bed with those four, part A, part C, part D, part E.

563
00:33:23,380 --> 00:33:25,270
Four parts, five hours.

564
00:33:25,270 --> 00:33:28,840
I want you to have those on repeat. Okay?

565
00:33:28,840 --> 00:33:30,870
That is crucial.

566
00:33:30,870 --> 00:33:38,290
To know the FOMC meeting, you know, the mandate, their opinion on employment, inflation.

567
00:33:38,290 --> 00:33:41,273
But you don't need to start going down into, oh,

568
00:33:41,273 --> 00:33:43,361
you know, lettuce, tomatoes, cucumbers. And that

569
00:33:43,361 --> 00:33:46,344
sort of detail, it starts to get a bit silly.

570
00:33:46,345 --> 00:33:47,400
That's what I'm saying.

571
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:51,440
I've seen people do silliness like that when I used to work on a trading floor.

572
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:58,680
I've seen people get way too obsessed and it's like, they're losing touch with reality.

573
00:33:58,680 --> 00:34:01,380
But it's better to do that than it is to

574
00:34:01,380 --> 00:34:04,350
do what most other people do, which is don't know anything.

575
00:34:04,350 --> 00:34:06,860
Okay, so... But I want to point this out to you one time,

576
00:34:06,860 --> 00:34:07,660
so at least you know.

